WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.180
A Bash script always runs as some identity.

2
00:00:04.280 --> 00:00:12.200
That identity may be an interactive user, a service account, a CI/CD runner, a scheduled job user, a container user, or root.

3
00:00:12.300 --> 00:00:18.220
Least privilege begins by asking which identity runs the script and what that identity can already do.

4
00:00:18.320 --> 00:00:20.800
The script is not only the lines in the file.

5
00:00:20.900 --> 00:00:24.900
It also includes the authority of the account that executes those lines.

6
00:00:25.180 --> 00:00:29.660
File permissions are a common source of overbroad access.

7
00:00:29.760 --> 00:00:38.080
Files and directories have owners, groups, modes, and sometimes inherited access patterns through parent directories or mounted storage.

8
00:00:38.180 --> 00:00:43.300
Groups are useful for shared operational access, but they should be specific enough to review.

9
00:00:43.400 --> 00:00:50.680
A broad group that quietly accumulates unrelated people and automation can become a hidden administrative boundary.

10
00:00:50.780 --> 00:00:59.020
If a script only needs one shared operational directory, avoid giving it access to a general-purpose group with far wider reach.

11
00:00:59.300 --> 00:01:01.300
Created files deserve attention too.

12
00:01:01.400 --> 00:01:09.400
Defaults such as umask can influence whether output files are readable by more users than intended.

13
00:01:09.500 --> 00:01:20.380
A script that produces sensitive reports, generated configuration, private metadata, or operational logs should set or verify restrictive permissions where appropriate.

14
00:01:20.480 --> 00:01:23.040
This is not only about confidentiality.

15
00:01:23.140 --> 00:01:24.900
It is also about integrity.

16
00:01:25.000 --> 00:01:32.600
If output is later consumed by another job, broad write permissions can turn a simple automation chain into a hard-to-review trust problem.

17
00:01:32.880 --> 00:01:38.080
Permission review should focus on the actual workflow, not only whether the script currently works.

18
00:01:38.180 --> 00:01:41.540
A script may work because it has access to everything.

19
00:01:41.640 --> 00:01:44.600
That is not evidence that it needs everything.

20
00:01:44.700 --> 00:01:52.540
Review the files it reads, the directories it writes, the commands it executes, and the users or groups that can alter its inputs.

21
00:01:52.640 --> 00:02:01.520
The goal is a permission model that supports the task while limiting accidental exposure, accidental modification, and unauthorized changes to the automation path.
